    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Atomic Structure: Senior Secondary|Quantum numbers and orbitals",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Quantum numbers are four labels that describe the state of an electron in an atom.\n\nAn orbital is a three-dimensional probability region that can hold at most two electrons.",
        "The principal number n identifies shell and energy, l identifies subshell shape, ml identifies orbital orientation, and ms identifies spin. Only allowed combinations describe possible electron states.\n\nOrbitals are not fixed paths. The s subshell has one orbital, p has three, d has five and f has seven; their shapes and orientations affect bonding.",
        "an electron in a 2p orbital has n = 2 and l = 1; ml can be -1, 0 or +1 and ms can be +1/2 or -1/2.\n\nExample: the three 2p orbitals have equal energy in an isolated atom and together can hold six electrons.",
        "n = 1,2,...; l = 0 to n-1; ml = -l to +l; ms = +/-1/2\n\nmaximum electrons in a subshell = 2(2l + 1)",
        "Do not assign l = 2 when n = 2; allowed l values must be smaller than n.\n\nDo not confuse an orbital with a shell; a shell contains one or more subshells and many orbitals.",
        "Quantum numbers organize electron configurations and help interpret spectra and periodic behavior.\n\nOrbital shapes explain bond direction, molecular geometry, spectra and periodic trends.",
        listOf("an electron in a 2p orbital has n = 2 and l = 1","ml can be -1, 0 or +1 and ms can be +1/2 or -1/2.","Example: the three 2p orbitals have equal energy in an isolated atom and together can hold six electrons.","n = 1,2,...; l = 0 to n-1; ml = -l to +l; ms = +/-1/2\n\nmaximum electrons in a subshell = 2(2l + 1)","The principal number n identifies shell and energy, l identifies subshell shape, ml identifies orbital orientation, and ms identifies spin.","Only allowed combinations describe possible electron states."),
        listOf("Orbitals","Electron configuration","Pauli principle","Probability clouds","Subshells","Hybridization","Quantum numbers")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Periodic Table and Trends|Periods, groups and blocks",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Groups are the vertical columns of the periodic table.\n\nPeriods are the horizontal rows of the periodic table.\n\np-Block elements have valence configuration ns2np1-6 and span metals, metalloids, non-metals and noble gases.",
        "Main-group elements in one group have similar valence-electron patterns and therefore often show similar chemical properties.\n\nElements in the same period have the same number of occupied main electron shells, while properties change gradually from left to right.\n\nGroup chemistry depends on both valence-electron count and the changing importance of d/f shielding and relativistic effects. A board-ready explanation should connect the stated relationship with electronic structure, a measurable trend, a balanced transformation or laboratory evidence, and should name important exceptions instead of treating the rule as absolute.",
        "lithium, sodium and potassium are Group 1 metals and commonly form +1 ions.\n\nExample: sodium through argon lie in Period 3 and have electrons occupying three main shells.\n\nExample: Across a period, increasing effective nuclear charge generally reduces size and increases non-metallic character. Show the governing relationship, apply the stated conditions and finish by interpreting the chemical result.",
        "same main group -> similar valence electrons and behavior\n\nperiod number -> number of occupied main shells\n\ngeneral valence configuration ns2np1-6",
        "Elements in a group are similar, not identical; reactivity can change down the group.\n\nElements in the same period do not usually have similar chemical properties.\n\nDo not answer p-Block electronic configuration and group trends from a memorised trend or name alone. Check the species, physical state, units, reaction conditions and known exceptions before drawing the conclusion.",
        "Groups help predict ion charges, formulas and reaction patterns.\n\nPeriod position helps predict electron arrangement and trends across the table.\n\np-Block electronic configuration and group trends supports prediction and problem solving in senior-secondary chemistry, laboratory work, materials, environmental decisions and industrial chemical processes.",
        listOf("lithium, sodium and potassium are Group 1 metals and commonly form +1 ions.","Example: sodium through argon lie in Period 3 and have electrons occupying three main shells.","Example: Across a period, increasing effective nuclear charge generally reduces size and increases non-metallic character.","Show the governing relationship, apply the stated conditions and finish by interpreting the chemical result.","same main group -> similar valence electrons and behavior\n\nperiod number -> number of occupied main shells\n\ngeneral valence configuration ns2np1-6","Main-group elements in one group have similar valence-electron patterns and therefore often show similar chemical properties."),
